  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the spinal cord do?",
      "a": "The spinal cord carries nerve messages between the brain and the body: sensations travel up it, and commands to muscles travel down it. It also handles some reflexes on its own.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the spinal cord"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a sensory nerve?",
      "a": "A sensory nerve carries messages from sense receptors, in the skin, muscles and organs, towards the spinal cord and brain. It tells the brain about touch, temperature, pain and body position.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the sensory nerve"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a motor nerve?",
      "a": "A motor nerve carries commands from the brain and spinal cord out to muscles and glands, telling muscles to contract.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the motor nerve"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What protects the spinal cord?",
      "a": "It is surrounded by the bones of the spine, the vertebrae, wrapped in three protective layers called meninges, and cushioned by a liquid called cerebrospinal fluid.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is the spinal cord inside the spine?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How fast do nerve messages travel?",
      "a": "The fastest nerve fibres carry signals at over 100 metres per second, so a message can travel from your toe to your brain in a small fraction of a second.",
      "asks": [
        "How quick are nerve signals?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The adult spinal cord is about 45 centimetres long and ends at about the first lumbar vertebra, at the top of the lower back.",
    "31 pairs of spinal nerves branch from the spinal cord.",
    "The fastest nerve fibres conduct signals at over 100 metres per second."
  ],
